Ladybird sizes a popup by measuring its document at 25×25 and taking the
root’s scroll size, up to 800×600, again after each layout that changed
something. Blink measures the same way when its
AutoSizeUsesScrollWidthForOverflow feature is on:
FrameViewAutoSizeInfo::AutoSizeIfNeeded() resets the frame to its
minimum size and reads the scroll width, at the first autosize, after
loading, and on layout or overflow changes, within the bounds Chromium’s
ExtensionPopup gives it.

A popup closes itself with window.close() whatever its history holds.
Chromium does that for all of an extension’s pages, since
extension_webkit_preferences.cc sets allow_scripts_to_close_windows for
them, and ExtensionHost::CloseContents() closes the host; Ladybird does
it for popups only.
